WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.700
- Hello, everyone, welcome back.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.400
I'm Rebecca Finlay, and
I am very pleased to be

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.150
moderating and leading
this final panel of the day

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:12.390
on AI policy.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.250
Bonjour a tous.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.160
One note, please remember you can join

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.510
into the conversation on social media

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.300
by tagging CIFAR news on Twitter,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.680
with the hashtag AICAN.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.770
And we are going to end the
panel with your questions.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.130
And so please submit
your questions through

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.380
the zoom Q&amp;A function,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.600
at the bottom of the screen

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.420
and I will try to get to them

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.090
at the end of the panel.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:42.450
The focus of the session
is on AI and Policy.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.500
And as Minister Shang
Pang affirmed so clearly,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.450
in his remarks this morning,

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.360
the government of Canada has committed

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.130
to developing and implementing policies

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.550
that promote trust

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.120
in a digital world and keep pace

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.150
with the rapid technological
development of AI.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.410
We know for example, about the launch

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.460
of the digital charter in May of 2019,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.000
and then the recent legislation
proposed through bill C-11,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.450
the digital charter implementation act

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.550
in November of 2020

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.120
alongside all sorts of
international efforts

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.960
including the Global Partnership on AI.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.500
And as the minister kindly
mentioned this morning,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.970
CIFAR's AI and society program.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.280
Part of the Pan-Canadian AI strategy,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.430
this program supports working groups

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.070
on a range of topics related

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.350
to social political and
economic impacts of AI.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.440
And most recently with
the generous partnership

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:41.270
of IDRC focusing on a call
for AI governance solutions

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.580
in low to middle income countries.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.030
Canada is fortunate.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.880
We have a rich community
of experts and advocates

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.130
committed to charting a path
to responsible AI governance.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.170
We have offices of information
and privacy commissioners

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.660
across the country,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.560
AI and social science
researchers and legal experts,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.810
as well as human rights and
civil society organizations

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.480
taking up the cause.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.770
And we are very lucky,
because we have some

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.770
of those leaders with
us today on this panel.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.430
So I am going to introduce them.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.010
And as I do, please take a moment to share

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.170
in the chat, the top
three things that come

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.560
to mind for you,

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.679
about AI policy in Canada.

00:02:23.679 --> 00:02:27.140
So please join me in welcoming four

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.780
remarkable individuals who are going

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.340
to join today's discussion.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.502
First, we have Golnoosh Farnadi.

00:02:32.502 --> 00:02:35.680
Golnoosh is the Canda CIFAR AI chair,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.200
a member of MILA,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.060
and an assistant
professor at HEC Montreal.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.210
She is a leader in algorithmic fairness

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.940
in machine learning, and decision-making

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.030
causality and interpretability.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:48.340
Great to have you with us Golnoosh.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.040
Also Patricia Kosseim.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.070
Patricia is Ontario's Information
and Privacy Commissioner,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.070
with a wealth of knowledge
in privacy and access law

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.970
and extensive experience in the public

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.480
private and health sectors.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.150
I for one think we are very lucky

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.320
to have you in Ontario, Patricia.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.600
So thanks so much for joining us.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.080
Then we have Deborah Raji.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.230
Deborah is a researcher
and a Mozilla fellow.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.810
In 2020 she was named one of
the top innovators under 35,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.400
by MIT technology review

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.210
for her research on racial and gender bias

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.540
in facial recognition systems.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.160
Deborah, great to have you with us.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.870
Thank you so much.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.240
And finally, we have Jacques Rajotte.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.010
Jacques is a lawyer and a leader

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.620
in International Development,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.280
who is currently the
Interim Executive Director

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.120
of the International Center
of Expertise in Montreal

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.810
for the advancement of AI, for
the global partnership on AI.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.470
Thanks so much Jacques.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.040
So let's get underway.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.060
I would really welcome

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.890
hearing from the four of you,

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.470
on sort of a, with a very broad question

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.550
just to reflect briefly
for us to get started

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.460
in sort of three minutes ish.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.600
Perhaps respond to the question,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.010
what do you think Canada
is doing well with respect

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.950
to AI policy and where do
you think we can improve?

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.700
And Commissioner Patricia,
why don't we start with you?

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.630
- Thank you Rebecca.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.360
And thank you for the
invitation to be here.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.620
I'm very honored

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.618
to be joining this panel of

00:04:16.618 --> 00:04:20.340
wonderful colleagues who am I've just had

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.360
the pleasure of meeting.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.620
It's always wonderful to meet new faces

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.590
and new people in this space.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.240
And so thank you for that opportunity.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.510
So, in terms of what Canada is doing well

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.370
in terms of AI leadership,

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.710
I'm gonna speak about privacy aspects

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.770
of that AI leadership in particular.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.210
And I would say that,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.450
from my perspective, I
think Canada is quite

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.020
an influential actor on
the international stage,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.400
punching way above its
weight, relatively speaking

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.810
in terms of privacy leadership.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.810
And to be clear, I'm not
talking about legal instruments

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.650
which are sorely lagging behind,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.960
but I'm talking about
concrete policy actions

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.550
that Canada has spearheaded.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.780
And there are several reasons for this.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.154
First, as you alluded to Rebecca,

00:05:06.154 --> 00:05:09.280
we have a federal system,
of course, here in Canada

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.410
which means we have 14 jurisdictions,

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.660
each with its own Commissioner
or data protection regulator.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.650
And as we say in French,
(speaks in foreign language)

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.920
this certainly strengthens our capacity,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.870
our involvement and our collective voice

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.300
on the international stage,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.490
and in particular, our role
in the global privacy assembly

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.590
which just this year, for instance

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:31.800
this past year

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.570
adopted a resolution

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:38.400
which our office co-sponsored
on accountability,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.543
responsible accountability in AI.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.400
Second, we've had a
really strong and steady

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.830
cadre of commissioners,

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.410
who by their own individual personalities,

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.960
interests and priorities have
taken a very proactive stance

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.460
in the international arena.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.220
And I'm thinking here for instance

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.860
of the influential concept
of privacy by design

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.600
that was minted by my
predecessor here in Ontario

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.360
and has become an integral
component of the EU GDPR,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.020
among other modern privacy
regimes around the world.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.150
The previous federal commissioner,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:18.000
has had a very influential
role in updating

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.450
the OECD guidelines

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.050
on privacy and security, and,

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.360
really was instrumental in
building the foundations

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.000
for international enforcement cooperation.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.910
We're seeing made possible today

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.030
between data protection
authorities around the globe.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:36.940
The current Federal Privacy
Commissioner has been, I mean,

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.540
very significant behind
the scenes contributions

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.950
to strengthening the governance structure

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.750
of the global privacy assembly.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:50.550
And one of our former
commissioners in Canada from BC,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:54.320
has gone on to take the
leadership of the UK

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:55.700
information commissioner's office

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.430
which is probably the largest DPA

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.280
or data protection affiliate in the world.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.530
Third I'd say, given the
absence of an overarching

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.520
federal privacy law in the U S

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.030
at least in the private sector,

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:10.570
this has really allowed
Canada to take a highly

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.597
credible persuasive leadership role,

00:07:12.597 --> 00:07:15.460
vis-a-vis our counterparts
internationally,

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.360
without being overshadowed
by our neighbors

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.800
to the South for change.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.580
And forth, I think we've
built a very strong

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.680
privacy research capacity in Canada.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:29.260
Thanks to some amazing research programs

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.390
you mentioned Rebecca and to name

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.960
I think some of them
would include, you know,

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.050
the large scale social science projects,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.970
we've seen dedicated to
privacy funded by Shirk,

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.400
the consumer interest
research program funded

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.210
by innovation science
and economic development,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:49.090
privacy research funded by

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.350
the office of the privacy commissioner.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.972
And not to mention the
interdisciplinary research

00:07:54.972 --> 00:07:58.190
programs prompted by CIA (murmurs)

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:01.130
ethics and knowledge translation programs.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.700
Genome Canada's integrated GELS programs,

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.640
and of course CIFAR's
AI and society program.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.940
So many of these opportunities have helped

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.280
our Canadian privacy
researchers gain visibility

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.330
on the international stage

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.193
and have some very significant influence.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.150
So, this is of course

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.700
as I said, you know,
concrete policy action.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:30.110
And while our actions have
been louder than words,

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.340
I think it's high time
for our words to catch up.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.330
And I'm talking specifically about

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.291
to the need to modernize
Canada's privacy statutes,

00:08:38.291 --> 00:08:39.950
but my three minutes are up.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:41.800
So I'll leave you in suspense.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.930
And I'm gonna talk a little bit more

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.943
about that later on in the panel.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.380
- Great, thank you, Patricia.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:52.960
And I love to think about the words

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.170
and the power of words
and the need for that.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.440
And when we think about
what we're doing today

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.290
in terms of bringing
these communities together

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:00.840
so thanks so much.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.380
- Deborah, would you like
to take a moment to reflect

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:05.123
on that question for us?

00:09:06.793 --> 00:09:08.193
- Yeah, for sure.

00:09:08.193 --> 00:09:11.670
I think I'm sort of probably
gonna take a little bit

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.790
of a broader lens to things,

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.530
in terms of to start off
things that I think Canada

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.160
is doing very well, and is very impressive

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.670
is just the agile policy
development especially with,

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:26.170
the algorithmic impact
assessments and that effort

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.340
incredibly at the department
of treasury I believe,

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.430
it's incredibly impressive
how sort of quickly

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.180
that group was able to
iterate on that idea

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.580
and how, you know,

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.220
that effort has really stood

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.930
as an example for a
lot of other countries.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.377
A lot of the work I do is in the U S

00:09:44.377 --> 00:09:45.690
and in the American context.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.113
And even in that context, it's really,

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.780
sort of a gold standard in terms of

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.560
quickly iterating on, you know,

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.630
documentation practice for policy purposes

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.760
and doing that, you know,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.569
at a pace that's not typical

00:10:01.569 --> 00:10:03.400
of policy development in general.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.270
So, yeah, very impressed with the

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.030
algorithmic impact assessment effort

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:10.120
and how quickly that was able to

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.530
be piloted, and how quickly
it was able to iterate.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:18.200
And also connecting that
with different stakeholders

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.850
in different ways to
really make that effective.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.000
So, yeah, that's definitely
something I think

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.520
Canada is doing very well.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.540
Also, Patricia already mentioned this

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.680
but I think the privacy by design effort

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.313
is also quite impressive.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.347
I do a lot of work in facial recognition

00:10:33.347 --> 00:10:35.790
and that work has been
incredibly influential

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:38.830
in terms of the policy
development in that space.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:39.950
You know, a lot of the standards

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.740
for data storage and data
distribution is sort of

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.730
derived from ideas of that work.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.560
So, I also agree that privacy
by design work in Canada

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.090
has been incredibly influential

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.680
and incredibly effective.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.570
In terms of, I guess,

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.770
things that are sort of opportunities

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.260
or things that Canada, you know,

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:09.260
might I guess benefit from
paying more attention to,

00:11:10.041 --> 00:11:12.230
you know, one that comes
to mind immediately is this

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:13.680
challenge of disclosure.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.083
So, and this is not a
Canadian specific thing, but,

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.860
you know, at the municipal
level, at the provincial level,

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.670
at the federal level,

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.747
it can be very difficult for anyone

00:11:24.747 --> 00:11:28.520
you know, a journalist,
a researcher, a regulator

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:32.520
a member of the public
to understand, you know,

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.540
the answer to two questions.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.470
One question being, if their
data is being collected

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:37.840
and how it's being collected or used

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.380
as part of some kind
of algorithmic process,

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.920
and then two, what kind of
algorithmic process is being used

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:46.600
as part of, you know, a
governmental bureaucratic process.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.053
And given that, you know,

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:52.233
Canada, as other countries, you know,

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.510
has a bunch of public institutions

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:59.040
that require transparency on these issues.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.720
It's become incredibly
challenging for researchers

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.060
even to get a sense of

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:08.220
how different departments or institutions,

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.430
public institutions even,

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.070
are making use of algorithms to make

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.620
very consequential decisions
about people's lives.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:17.440
So, as an example of something
I can think of, you know

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.400
the Toronto police was recently discovered

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:20.880
to be using Clearview AI,

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.530
a facial recognition tool,

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:24.910
that's very invasive.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:28.108
And it was revealed as part of,

00:12:28.108 --> 00:12:32.710
you know, a journalist looking
into the Clearview AI vendor.

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:34.660
And it wasn't anything that was disclosed

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:37.980
by Toronto police to the public, or,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.660
you know, even to any other
sort of accountability board.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.320
So there's a lot of
challenges with disclosure,

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.750
and with open and transparent
communication around,

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.130
when an algorithm is being used,

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:50.070
and if it affects you,

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.350
and if it's using your
data and things like that.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.860
And I think that's a huge
challenge that Canada

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.400
also has to face as well.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.520
And then I will say the other sort of

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:03.210
potential challenge that
Canadians will probably

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.010
need to encounter with
respect to policy making,

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:09.790
is also really having open
and honest conversations

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.249
around anti-racism, and
some of the issues with,

00:13:12.249 --> 00:13:15.400
you know, with respect to

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:19.290
you know, the societal
dynamics that contribute

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:22.780
to some of the injustice
perpetuated by these algorithms.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.110
That's something that in the U S,

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:28.200
just due to the weight of their history,

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:31.390
it's sort of a conversation
that flows a little bit easier,

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:33.800
and people are a little
bit more informed about it.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.420
Whereas they find sometimes in Canada

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:37.970
it can be challenging to

00:13:38.954 --> 00:13:41.680
allow the conversation to get
to that point with respect

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.630
to algorithmic deployment
and how that connects

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:47.063
to the way that algorithms
are designed and deployed.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:49.110
So, yeah, that's what I would say

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:51.390
would maybe be a couple of challenges

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.660
to look forward to (chuckles),

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.610
in addition to the stuff that
Canada is doing very well at.

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:57.420
- Thank you so much.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:00.290
Yes, much, much, much more work to be done

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:01.500
on both of those fronts.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.083
So thank you so much Deborah.

00:14:04.591 --> 00:14:06.550
