the mission of the GPAI.

00:17:56.170 --> 00:18:01.100
Also I'll add comments too
what Golnoosh just said.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:02.930
But I think what's important
to understand first

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:05.520
in terms of the mission of the GPAI,

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:09.020
it's not directly focused
on developing AI policy,

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.260
because we have the privilege
of having has as a partner,

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:14.530
the OECD as part of the GPIA

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.160
and that's where the secretary
of the GPIA is situated.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.790
But we are actually a bot
end of the policymaking.

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.520
First, you know, the mission of the GP is,

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:25.960
to move from theory to practice.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:28.260
So we are developing solution

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:30.360
or experimenting potential solution

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:34.360
that will eventually
inform a policy maker.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.640
And once those policies have been adopted,

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:38.350
then we are also at the other end,

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.430
trying to develop business models,

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:43.090
so that we will be able to implement that.

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.497
The way that this has been done

00:18:44.497 --> 00:18:45.750
and in terms of talking about

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.840
the leadership of Canada,

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:50.110
for those who are not aware,

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:51.980
the Global Partnership on AI is,

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:55.400
initially an initiative
of Canada and France,

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:59.830
started through a transition
to the G7 presidency.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.990
And I think it's another
great illustration

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.000
of how Canada is taking
a leadership position

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:06.760
on the adoption of responsibility.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.400
I mean, everybody's aware of
the declaration of Montreal,

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.080
resonate quite a lot on
the international scene.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.700
So I think that Canada
has a lot of credibility,

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.160
and the GP is one more example of how

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.730
our commitment to make
a strong contribution

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:25.403
to the development of responsible AI.

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.900
In terms of the specific
elements of the mission,

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.180
I think I would like
to link to the comments

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:33.780
on the international side.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.850
I think that the mission of the GP is

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:37.880
not only to ensure

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:42.030
the adoption of responsible
AI, but to making sure

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.880
that it is done for the benefit of all

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.890
and more specifically in collaboration

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:48.610
and taking into consideration,

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:51.940
the interest of emerging
and developing countries.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:54.533
So part of the mission of
GP will be to make sure

00:19:54.533 --> 00:19:56.570
that we are inclusive of the voice

00:19:56.570 --> 00:20:01.240
of the developing country,

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.340
but, we as (murmurs) and
I'll maybe I'll spend just

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:06.900
10 seconds explaining what
the center of expertise

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:10.610
boost the abbreviation that is (murmurs).

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.100
we are actually supporting
some of the expert group.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.330
And one of them being the
responsible working group,

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.940
through their work to make sure they have

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:20.480
all the back office and the proper support

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.740
to be able to deliver on their ambitions

00:20:24.427 --> 00:20:28.940
to deliver the impact as well
of the Global partnership.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:32.520
But part of what we do
also will be to incorporate

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:35.260
within our own team,
international researcher

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:37.340
who comes from the developing countries.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.616
So we have been in discussion
over the last few months

00:20:40.616 --> 00:20:43.510
with the various government
institution in Canada,

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:45.870
to make sure we have the
proper financial support

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:46.703
to do that,

00:20:46.703 --> 00:20:49.560
but that will be one of
the important contribution

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:50.660
also of the (murmurs),

00:20:51.595 --> 00:20:54.816
to the positioning of the GP.

00:20:54.816 --> 00:20:57.085
So I think that we'll
continue that conversation.

00:20:57.085 --> 00:20:58.944
There's so much more to talk about

00:20:58.944 --> 00:21:01.027
the Global Partnership
and the role of senior

00:21:01.027 --> 00:21:03.967
within the Canadian AI ecosystem.

00:21:03.967 --> 00:21:06.770
(murmurs) It's an illustration
of our leadership.

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:09.823
It's linked from before and
after the policy-making,

00:21:09.823 --> 00:21:13.640
so it's very much part of a
continuum of implementation.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:17.630
And I can answer more
questions about specific work

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:20.720
that we will be focusing
on for the coming year.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:22.620
- Thank you Jacques, and I
know we'll get some questions

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.150
in the Q&amp;A for more information
about some of those areas.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:28.920
So thanks, we'll look into
that later in the panel.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.030
Thank you all.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:33.540
I think that really helps
to set the stage for,

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.590
some of the questions
that I think as a group

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:38.060
we would like to dive into a little bit.

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:40.540
And one of the things
that I've found useful

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.000
in thinking about AI and
AI policy is to really

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.750
understand specific use cases,

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.430
or specific sectors to really understand

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:51.560
what regulatory regimes
might already be in place,

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:54.130
but also what the policy
challenges might be.

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:57.500
So why don't we start with one of those.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:00.140
Let's dig into the question
of facial recognition

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.520
or surveillance technologies a little bit.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:04.500
It's clearly top of mind,

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:07.075
we saw it last week in terms of

00:22:07.075 --> 00:22:11.470
the events in in Washington
that's very controversial.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:13.380
There are all sorts of
initiatives underway

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:16.180
from all sorts of different
policy perspectives.

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:18.240
So I think the question
I'd be interested is,

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:22.890
what do you think policymakers
need to understand?

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.670
What is it about this
particular technology

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:27.120
that is problematic?

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.706
And, what if any guidance,

00:22:29.706 --> 00:22:30.830
would you give them

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:32.360
as they start to think about

00:22:33.273 --> 00:22:35.250
how this technology moving forward?

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:37.413
And Deborah why don't we start with you?

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:42.380
- Yeah, sure my favorite
topic (laughs loudly).

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:46.420
Yeah I think there's a lot of

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:49.850
really important things that
policymakers need to understand

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:51.260
about facial recognition.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:53.633
Something that I start
off with is that it is,

00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:59.300
you know, often grouped in
with other biometric tools,

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:02.840
such as fingerprinting
and other forms of sort of

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:04.430
being able to track humans

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:06.840
and surveil them using
their biometric information.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.870
But there's a couple
interesting differences

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:10.270
with facial recognition.

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:12.280
So for one thing,

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:15.850
facial recognition almost
requires no interaction.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.500
So my, you know, I can...

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:22.220
I don't upload my finger
print onto social media,

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.940
and I need to interact
with a particular device

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:26.640
in order for my fingerprint to be,

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.090
you know, uploaded somewhere.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:32.200
Whereas with facial
recognition, we're very casual

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.680
with the way that we handle our face data.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:38.340
We treat it differently
than we do other biometrics.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:39.800
We're much less careful and that's

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:41.900
you can find someone's face quite easily

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.520
and that can be included
in any kind of database.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:47.100
And also as I'm walking
through the street, you know,

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:48.850
a facial recognition camera can capture me

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:50.590
and it doesn't necessarily
have to notify me

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:54.700
that I'm now, you know,
captured and my image processed.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:57.040
So there's a lot of
challenges with respect

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:58.710
to the lack of direct interaction

00:23:58.710 --> 00:23:59.760
with facial recognition tools,

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:02.300
that makes consent very, very challenging.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:05.370
The other thing about
facial recognition is that,

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:09.258
starting in about 2014
with the deep face model

00:24:09.258 --> 00:24:11.620
that was developed by Facebook,

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:14.480
deep learning has been
sort of the primary method

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:16.547
of training models for facial recognition.

00:24:16.547 --> 00:24:21.040
And that means that there's
this enormous data requirement

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:23.270
in order for modern day
facial recognition tools

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:25.060
to work effectively.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:27.540
What this means is that
there's pretty much databases

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.830
of you know, millions of faces
required in order to train

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:33.290
in a really effective model to
detect particular attributes

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:36.900
or to, you know, a database
that is much larger than

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:39.260
you know, what you might
need for other sort

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.530
of biometric tools using
other types of methods.

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:45.700
So as a technology, it is quite particular

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:47.000
there are characteristics

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.760
of facial recognition that,

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:51.010
position it to be especially harmful

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:53.440
and challenging and dangerous.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:56.100
And with respect to sort
of the policy directions

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:58.697
that I hope people take
with facial recognition,

00:24:58.697 --> 00:25:01.966
you know, one direction that we saw,

00:25:01.966 --> 00:25:04.570
I used to be a fellow
at the AI Now Institute

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:06.610
in New York university and,

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:08.450
a lot of the work that happened

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:11.010
in that space was around disclosure

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:12.180
like I had mentioned before.

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:15.260
Just understanding when facial
recognition is being used,

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:17.320
and enforcing a requirement where

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:20.040
if my face is included in a database,

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:21.293
that's then analyzed,

00:25:22.538 --> 00:25:25.890
if my face is captured
in a way that, you know,

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:27.980
I'm not aware of, you know,
how do I get notified?

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:32.970
If the NYPD or another sort
of policing groups such as,

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:33.803
like I mentioned,

00:25:33.803 --> 00:25:35.830
Toronto police was caught
using Clearview AI.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:39.750
So if there's a institution
that affects me, you know,

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.224
how can I be included in
the process of, you know,

00:25:42.224 --> 00:25:46.400
asserting my rights and
being even notified that,

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.430
you know, my police department
is using facial recognition.

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:51.760
So the simple question of disclosure

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:54.440
was something that had to be
really fought for in New York.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.730
What that ended up being was the post act,

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.320
which was pretty much
an effort to force NYPD,

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:03.040
to disclose what kind of
surveillance technology

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:04.370
that they were using.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:07.740
So I think that's an
undervalued policy strategy

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:10.950
is to just encourage
transparency and communication

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:12.580
with the general public about,

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:13.780
how an algorithm is being used

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:15.110
and when it's being used.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:16.950
And then outside of that,

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.100
something that's also
not very well considered

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:22.340
or often ignored is this
idea of performance.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:24.830
And that's been a lot of my work has been,

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:27.550
auditing these technologies
for performance

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:29.360
on different demographic groups.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.610
So we've already seen in the U S you know,

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:34.190
at least two or three cases of individuals

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.646
with darker skin tone being misidentified

00:26:36.646 --> 00:26:38.350
by facial recognition tools

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:41.510
and having their lives
really negatively impacted

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:42.660
as a result.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:46.170
And then I guess the like
very final thing I'll say is,

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.680
and this is sort of,

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:52.846
the next wave of policy
interventions that we saw

00:26:52.846 --> 00:26:56.760
after the audit work really became public,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:58.670
was a move for restriction.

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:01.420
So, you know, there's
particular institutions

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:04.310
like I mentioned, police
departments is a good candidate

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:07.520
but also, you know, there's
been great work by the

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:10.420
The Citizen Lab at University of Toronto

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:12.830
that's done great work into
the use of facial recognition

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.140
and immigration processes
and things like that.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.370
So there's a lot of cases where,

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:18.980
perhaps facial recognition is a tool

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:20.700
that should not be used ever.

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:23.380
So a lot of proposals for moratoriums,

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:27.820
and bans for particular
individuals or institutions

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.150
use of facial recognition
in a particular context

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:32.180
that's deemed, you know, too high stakes

00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:37.046
or too sensitive for that
kind of technology to be used.

00:27:37.046 --> 00:27:39.915
So yeah, I think those are
like three policy directions

00:27:39.915 --> 00:27:42.696
that I often speak about

00:27:42.696 --> 00:27:45.155
that I hope Canada

00:27:45.155 --> 00:27:47.163
definitely embraces moving forward.

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:50.070
- Thank you so much.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:52.973
Others who'd like to comment, Patricia.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:56.410
- Yeah, I just thought that was excellent.

00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:57.317
Thank you, Deborah.

00:27:57.317 --> 00:28:00.990
I wanted to just add a little
bit of a postscript to,

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:03.303
the story about Clearview AI,

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:05.800
and comment a little bit

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:10.670
on the specific use case
that you presented Rebecca.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.383
As some of you may know,

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:17.130
and Deborah alluded to this,

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:18.525
it came to light,

00:28:18.525 --> 00:28:22.570
the assistance that
Clearview AI was providing to

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:26.880
police services across
Canada came to light and,

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:30.980
it led to an investigation
that is being conducted

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:33.510
by the Federal Commissioner
along with Quebec,

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:34.674
BC and Alberta,

00:28:34.674 --> 00:28:37.250
are conducting a joint investigation.

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:39.020
Interestingly Clearview AI

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:46.510
pulled back and ceased
providing it services in Canada,

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:49.460
but the investigation is ongoing because

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:52.060
of the importance of,

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.870
producing a report,

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:58.080
given the stakes for Canadians privacy.

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:00.270
So it will be very interesting to watch.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:01.103
I just wanna say

00:29:01.103 --> 00:29:04.770
it's not the first investigation
on this specific use case.

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.600
If you recall, remember there
were the Vancouver riots

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:11.940
of 2011 following the Stanley Cup loss.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:14.770
And, on the evening of that game

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:17.200
there were riots and many fans

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:21.290
riding in the streets of downtown
Vancouver had taken photos

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:25.730
and uploaded those to websites,
Facebook pages, et cetera.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:29.620
And the police ensued with
an investigation that,

00:29:29.620 --> 00:29:32.780
included the collection of
thousands of these images.

00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:36.460
And the insurance corporation
of British Columbia had

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:38.890
developed FRT system,

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:42.140
originally developed for
reducing fraudulent acquisition

00:29:42.140 --> 00:29:44.490
and use of driver's licenses

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:47.110
as part of the insurance process.

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:49.230
And they offered to assist the police

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:51.623
with the use of this technology,

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:54.840
and they had no compulsion to do so.

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:57.803
There was no subpoena, no
warrant, no court order,

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:00.610
and it led to an investigation

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:03.040
by the information and
privacy commissioner of BC.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:04.660
And this was more than a decade ago.

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:06.990
And what's interesting
there was, you know,

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:09.790
one of the key issues that
the Commissioner raised

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:11.680
was the shift in use,

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:16.100
from a technology that had
been developed for the use

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:20.790
of detecting fraudulent
use of driver's licenses,

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:25.700
to actually investigating
criminal behavior post riot,

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:28.110
without any knowledge or
consent of the individuals,

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:30.500
let alone any adequate notice to citizens,

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:31.750
that this was being done.

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:35.550
And so I just wanted to point that out

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:38.430
'cause that is an interesting
use case here in Canada

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:39.700
more than a decade ago.

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:41.296
And the commissioner had this to say

00:30:41.296 --> 00:30:45.010
about the use of this technology

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.050
without judicial authorization saying,

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:49.370
judicial oversight is necessary to ensure

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:53.770
that any changing use of this
magnitude is proportional

00:30:53.770 --> 00:30:55.920
to the public good served
by the infringement

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.850
on privacy rights of citizens.

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:00.470
So there's a lot of empathy
and sympathy for the use

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:04.140
of FRT in the context in
which we saw that, you know,

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:07.820
the riots in the Capitol,

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:11.120
but I think there are also
compelling reasons to make sure

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:15.370
that the guardrails and, you know,

00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:18.570
respect of human rights and civil rights

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:22.043
are protected in the process.

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:25.573
- Thank you Patricia.

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:29.970
Maybe I'll move on to another question

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:32.696
because I think both Deborah and Patricia,

00:31:32.696 --> 00:31:36.000
your comments and advice with regards to

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:38.330
facial recognition and
surveillance technologies

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:40.410
really speaks to data.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.820
And the way in which AI, and
AI systems like deep learning

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:48.820
use data and the requirement
have governments and

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:54.080
for companies to understand
how that data is used

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:56.440
and how it is used
differently when we think

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:58.720
about in terms of AI systems.

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:01.300
So I really would be
interested maybe beginning

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:03.760
with you Golnoosh to dig
into the question of,

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:06.010
what is it that you really
think that policy makers

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:10.180
need to know about how AI
systems use data differently

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:14.760
than other big data or
data analytics programming

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:16.890
and what should they be
considering when they think

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:18.998
about the use of AI in terms of either

00:32:18.998 --> 00:32:20.990
products and services to citizens,

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:25.683
or private sector products
and services to customers?

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:29.160
- I want to also mention something about

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.240
like the applications that we discussed,

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:32.240
the face recognition.

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:34.790
It's not the only application,

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:38.070
that we see discrimination is happening.

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:41.490
So currently, like AI tools

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:44.420
are becoming part of the modern society.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.240
So we are like, people are using it

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:48.340
in different scenarios every day.

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:50.850
Even the face recognition
is just not harmful

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:52.557
that you're using it in
law enforcement and policy.

00:32:52.557 --> 00:32:54.610
And you can actually use it in like,

00:32:54.610 --> 00:32:56.250
when people are coming to the door

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:58.304
and recognizing their
face and open the door.

00:32:58.304 --> 00:32:59.640
The simple scenario as that,

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.480
it could be discriminatory, right?

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:06.480
So the issue is coming like
partially coming from a data

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:09.990
because we are living
in the era of big data.

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:11.980
We have access to large
amounts of datasets.

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:14.580
So from the social networks we have,

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.750
from the mobile data, we
have sensors, et cetera.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:20.030
And also we have easy to use tools,

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:22.130
AI tools at the moment.

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:24.030
And the combination of these two,

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:26.480
means that we are creating
different applications,

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:28.560
different domains that people are using

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:30.620
without really considering
the consequences

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:32.450
of like these kinds of applications.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:33.770
And now we have seen

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:36.520
like many negative impacts of those cases.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.890
Then the issue is that like people are

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:41.000
or the companies are using this kind

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.513
of tools are not really aware

00:33:42.513 --> 00:33:44.050
of like the data that we are using.

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:46.240
So they are not looking at the data

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:47.307
and the breakdown of the data

00:33:47.307 --> 00:33:50.410
and maybe the respective
different sub groups.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:53.920
So we have discrimination
partially coming from the fact

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:57.260
that we don't have the
representation of the population

00:33:57.260 --> 00:33:59.610
in the data when we are
using these kinds of tools,

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:01.420
for the general population.

00:34:01.420 --> 00:34:03.140
So we have like one group

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:06.010
that the model is trained based on it,

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:08.890
and we are applying to all
the groups in the society.

00:34:08.890 --> 00:34:11.090
So that's one of the
issues that we can see

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:13.700
but, I want to mention like discrimination

00:34:13.700 --> 00:34:15.860
is not just because of the data.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:19.170
So models can learn those
kind of behaviors from a data

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:20.570
because of the stereotypes,

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:23.030
because of the historical discrimination

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:24.740
and can actually amplify it.

00:34:24.740 --> 00:34:27.300
So it's not just learning
it and doing the same thing,

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:28.610
it can actually amplify it.

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:31.100
But unfortunately, I should say that

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:33.700
like the way that these
kind of models are designed

00:34:33.700 --> 00:34:36.860
they could actually
introduce biases as well.

00:34:36.860 --> 00:34:38.960
So it's not the fact that like,

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.210
if you are cleaning the data,

00:34:40.210 --> 00:34:41.760
if you don't have any issue with the data,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:44.300
the model itself is going to be clean.

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:46.460
So we have to consider that discrimination

00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:47.560
can happen at both sides

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:50.119
and that's why we need the solutions

00:34:50.119 --> 00:34:51.543
with respect to that.

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:57.053
I also wanted to mention about that,

00:34:57.890 --> 00:34:59.700
the issue about AI.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:03.570
AI is a kind of a vague term.

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:07.400
The problem is that fairness by definition

00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:09.470
is interdisciplinary and also subjective.

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:10.570
So in different domains

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:12.820
we have to actually
define what does it mean

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:14.441
that is discriminatory.

00:35:14.441 --> 00:35:17.760
And we do have law against
discrimination, of course.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:19.990
But the issue is that it's designed

00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:22.152
to be vague (laughs loudly).

00:35:22.152 --> 00:35:25.890
So it's a law for
different interpretations

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.560
but then you're coming to the case of AI,

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:31.210
as a researcher, as a computer scientist,

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:34.500
you actually need a definition
which is deterministic.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:36.260
And that's why this kind of interpretation

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:39.630
of coming from a legal
document into the AI system

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:40.560
it's kind of hard.

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.200
So we know we have issues with data.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.810
We know we have issues with AI tools

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.200
with respect to discrimination,

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:49.270
but coming up with a
solution for that is going

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:50.190
to be very hard,

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:52.060
because of it is very subjective.

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:54.550
You have to define it for
different disciplines,

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.050
different stakeholders, what does it mean

00:35:57.050 --> 00:35:58.410
that it's fair or not,

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.180
and that's the hard problem to solve.

00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:02.360
But it doesn't necessarily mean that

00:36:02.360 --> 00:36:04.020
we cannot solve it (chuckles),

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:08.370
because the issue that we are
facing is the issue of today.

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:10.060
And if you are not finding the solution

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:12.470
even a good solution,
not the perfect solution,

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.120
today we are actually facing
the systematic discrimination

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:18.880
which is far worse than the discrimination

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:20.820
that we had before the society.

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:22.660
We are dealing with

00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:26.601
the harm that is going to be systematic.

00:36:26.601 --> 00:36:28.751
And we have to actually
deal with it today.

00:36:30.690 --> 00:36:33.180
- Thank you, would others like to comment

00:36:33.180 --> 00:36:36.050
on those issues that Golnoosh has raised

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:39.500
before we move into a
Q&amp;A with the audience?

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:42.603
- Yeah, maybe just very briefly
if you allow me Rebecca.

00:36:43.742 --> 00:36:47.900
Just to connect maybe to
the international context

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:49.300
the one that

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.787
I'm living in through
the GP and (murmurs),

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:56.640
but I think that those biases
that have been referred to,

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:57.990
also in the way...

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:00.020
Especially when we talk
about the developing

00:37:00.020 --> 00:37:02.164
or emerging countries,

00:37:02.164 --> 00:37:04.890
you know, there are a lot
of those when they implement

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:09.310
or try to implement some of
those technology processes

00:37:09.310 --> 00:37:11.710
you know, in, in developing countries,

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:15.180
the level of bias, you know,

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:18.410
the risk of bias has
increased because of the fact

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:21.010
that there was a lack of
input data in that process.

00:37:21.010 --> 00:37:22.320
So that's one element.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:24.550
The other element also which is

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.533
one that could have an influence is

00:37:26.533 --> 00:37:29.980
the difference of the
cultural environment in which,

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:32.400
you know, some of those
technologies are being implemented

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:34.410
and the implication that they might have

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:35.900
or the perception it might have

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:37.580
from a cultural point of view also.

00:37:37.580 --> 00:37:40.910
I think that when the GP was designed,

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:42.620
the idea is that first,

00:37:42.620 --> 00:37:46.550
in terms of having as
many voices as possible

00:37:46.550 --> 00:37:48.530
represented at the table,
it's a multi-sectorial.

00:37:48.530 --> 00:37:50.840
So we have private sector, civil society

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:54.260
as well as scientists and academics

00:37:54.260 --> 00:37:55.300
are at the table,

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:56.780
so that all of the point of view...

00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:58.890
But the idea is also as I mentioned before

00:37:58.890 --> 00:38:00.270
is to bring at the table

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.370
the voices of developing
countries and emerging countries,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.550
so that these are developed thinking

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:05.900
taking into consideration the fact

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:07.240
that they might need to be adapted

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:09.410
and it's already built in, you know,

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.780
in the design that they will be adaptable

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:13.650
to those specific contexts recognizing

00:38:13.650 --> 00:38:15.300
where those differences might be.

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:18.563
- Thank you, Deb?

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:22.030
- Yeah I just had a quick addition

00:38:22.030 --> 00:38:25.600
which was another sort of dimension of

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.850
data's relationship with like AI,

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:29.990
especially modern tools which,

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:31.960
lean heavily on machine
learning and deep learning

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:35.960
in particular is just the
resource allocation required.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:38.977
So, you know, data sets used
in modern machine learning

00:38:38.977 --> 00:38:41.640
and AI systems are very, very large,

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:43.020
and that requires,

00:38:43.020 --> 00:38:45.130
it's very resource
intensive to train a model

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:46.410
and deploy a model.

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:49.450
And as a result of that there's
very specific power dynamics

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:51.050
that come out of that.

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:53.170
There's a lot of environmental risk,

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:54.990
but also it's really hard to control,

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:57.694
very, very large datasets

00:38:57.694 --> 00:39:01.180
and things that are sort of only,

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.450
I guess projects and a
culture, which we do have

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:07.530
in machine learning of
just only focusing on,

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:09.410
collecting as much data as possible

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:11.500
leads to a lot of the
mistakes that were mentioned

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:13.790
by the others around not doing a good job

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:15.530
adapting to a specific domains,

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:18.330
not doing a good job
representing the population

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:19.930
that it's gonna be deployed on.

00:39:19.930 --> 00:39:21.276
And this is, you know,

00:39:21.276 --> 00:39:24.184
one of the causes of this

00:39:24.184 --> 00:39:28.290
is definitely just the
sheer volume of data

00:39:28.290 --> 00:39:30.483
usually required for
these systems to work.

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:33.140
- Good point thank you.

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:35.120
Okay, so let's turn to
one of the questions

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:35.953
from the audience.

00:39:35.953 --> 00:39:39.100
I'm actually gonna pull a
couple together here because,

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:41.260
this really interesting thing
we've been talking about,

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:44.490
the rapid advancement and
innovations in the technology

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:46.560
and policy and the difference in speed.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:50.040
And so the question of,
how can policy be used to

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:53.300
advance innovation to
actually support innovation

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.170
to create a policy
environment where there is

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:57.450
responsible innovation is one

00:39:57.450 --> 00:39:58.910
of the questions that's come in

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:00.484
and whether that touches on data

00:40:00.484 --> 00:40:02.670
or international efforts or otherwise.

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:03.660
And I wonder, Patricia,

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:06.290
can you talk a little bit
about that in terms of

00:40:06.290 --> 00:40:09.320
how you see that interplay
between policy and innovation

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.053
and the role of the commissioner?

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:15.130
- Sure, I think there's

00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:20.180
an important role for
different forms of policy.

00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:22.380
Deborah talked about
agile forms of policy,

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:23.350
I'll speak to in a moment.

00:40:23.350 --> 00:40:26.820
But I think at the very basic level,

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:29.970
there is a foundation of statutory policy

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:33.740
that's required to set
the level playing field

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:36.760
for all competitors, to play fair,

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:40.250
responsibly and to spirit on innovation.

00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:44.610
So you talked about, for instance, C-11.

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:48.210
C-11 is a much needed
effort, federal bill,

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:51.720
that's been tabled to modernize
the federal act PIPEDA,

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:54.550
which is lagging behind in many respects

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:58.420
in terms of its capacity to deal with,

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:03.330
some of the challenges of
artificial intelligence,

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:05.770
data analytics that were never anticipated

00:41:05.770 --> 00:41:08.163
when it was adopted
more than 20 years ago.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:13.690
One of the big advantages of PIPEDA

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:17.040
was that it was principles
based, from the very outset

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.540
which allowed it to survive this long

00:41:19.540 --> 00:41:22.240
but even those principles
have been overtaken

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:24.230
and is in need of modernization.

00:41:24.230 --> 00:41:25.063
The good news is,

00:41:25.063 --> 00:41:28.530
C-11 is principle based as well.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:31.580
There are provisions in
there that deal specifically

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:34.710
with automated decision systems.

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:37.880
So there's certainly some really important

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:40.740
modernizations there that will help

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:44.420
spur on innovation and
provide predictability

00:41:44.420 --> 00:41:47.770
for innovators in this space.

00:41:47.770 --> 00:41:48.630
So that's a good thing.

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:50.890
There's a flexible mechanisms for instance

00:41:50.890 --> 00:41:54.170
of permitting consent exemptions,

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:58.580
for responsible use
and collection of data.

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:02.810
And along with that comes
enhanced transparency requirements

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:05.220
stronger accountability mechanisms,

00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:07.400
the right to an explanation,

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:08.840
the possibility to

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:14.650
also spur stronger enforcement

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:16.630
triggering stronger
enforcement mechanisms.

00:42:16.630 --> 00:42:19.130
So all of that is part of a package.

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:21.350
I think from a statutory perspective

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:22.990
that's important to form the base.

00:42:22.990 --> 00:42:25.603
But along with that,
what's important also is

00:42:25.603 --> 00:42:30.510
that the bill allows for
other forms of policy.

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:33.590
More agile responses such
as codes of practice,

00:42:33.590 --> 00:42:36.860
certification programs,
where industry itself

00:42:36.860 --> 00:42:41.380
the innovators itself can
develop sector specific guidance

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:43.500
that with the approval
of the commissioner,

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.240
then allow them as long as they comply

00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:48.880
with that code approved or
that commissioner approved code

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:51.500
of practice within the
certification program,

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:53.400
they are given or provided certain level

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:58.400
of immunity from downstream
penalties that could be imposed.

00:42:58.630 --> 00:43:02.720
So this is the kind of complimentary forms

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:07.720
of industry specific compliance mechanisms

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:11.840
that I think are very important
in an innovative space.

00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:15.240
The other forms of self
regulatory mechanisms

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:18.657
are the data privacy
impact assessments or PIAs,

00:43:18.657 --> 00:43:20.956
or the algorithmic impact assessments

00:43:20.956 --> 00:43:22.530
in the space of AI

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:26.900
that are incredibly important
and indispensable part

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:30.550
of a robust privacy management
program, where again

00:43:30.550 --> 00:43:33.810
innovators can take
matters in their own hands,

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:36.090
in order to, you know,

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:40.390
develop the baseline that
will engender the trust needed

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:43.670
to enable adoption of these technologies.

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:45.910
So I'll leave it at that.

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:49.036
I can talk for hours, but I think that

00:43:49.036 --> 00:43:50.020
I think we need both.

00:43:50.020 --> 00:43:53.640
I think we need statute based
law that forms the principles

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:54.700
the baseline principles,

00:43:54.700 --> 00:43:56.810
and we need these mechanisms

00:43:56.810 --> 00:43:59.770
for compliance initiatives

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:02.940
from the industry sectors themselves,

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:05.283
to compliment those statutes.

00:44:06.490 --> 00:44:07.323
- Thank you so much.

00:44:07.323 --> 00:44:09.577
I really do feel like
we could keep talking

00:44:09.577 --> 00:44:11.760
for much more time.

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:13.790
There's many more questions

00:44:13.790 --> 00:44:16.560
that we all have and lots
more information to share.

